Description: Wayland: give a window two views while an engine draws it as a pair
 .
 Stereo KWin shows depth inside a surface when a toolkit draws the surface's
 elements once per eye and hands the two views over as the desktop's own depth.
 For a raster window the backing store holds both eyes; an OpenGL window needs
 the EGL surface to have them. An engine that draws a window once per eye, as
 the edition's Qt Quick does, now asks for them with the window's
 _q_stereo_views set to 2. At the start of the next frame, never between the
 two parts of one, the window's EGL surface is created again with two views
 (EGL_EXT_multiview_window), which the EGL implementation declares as a full
 side-by-side pair, and the window reports a stereo format; the same first
 commit marks the pair, with kde_stereo_depth_v1, as the desktop's depth rather
 than stereo content. When the engine asks for one view again, the surface is
 made again with one, and the mark goes with the same commit as the EGL
 implementation's declaration.
 .
 A window that asked for stereo buffers itself keeps its two views either way;
 only the mark changes. QWaylandStereoDepth may now be called from a window's
 render thread.
